A port of xv6-riscv to the Milk-V Duo 256M (Sophgo SG2002). xv6 boots from
the SD card through the board's own U-Boot and uses the UART0 console.

Peripheral drivers for gpio, pwm, i2c and spi are included, along with some
small user programs that demo them.

Dependencies:

- RISC-V gcc toolchain (riscv64-unknown-elf- or riscv64-linux-gnu-)
- qemu-system-riscv64
- mkimage (u-boot-tools) and genimage

Build for milkv duo:

```bash
./build_duo256m.sh
```
The image is written to `imgtools/xv6-duo256m-sd.img`. Burn it to an SD card:
```bash
sudo dd if=imgtools/xv6-duo256m-sd.img of=/dev/sdX bs=4M conv=fsync
```
Make sure `sdX` is really the SD card that you want to write to.

Build for qemu:

```bash
git checkout kernel/config.h
make qemu
```

gpio demo:

```bash
blink 5 # blinks the onboard LED 5 times
```

`button` waits for presses on GP15 (pin 20) and toggles the onboard LED on
each one. It runs until killed, so start it with `&`.

```bash
button &
button: level 1
press 1
press 2
```

pwm demo:
PWM6 on GP3 (pin 5). Connect an LED with a resistor (I used
330 ohm) from GP3 to GND. Arguments are period and duty in ns.

```bash
pwm 1000000 900000
pwm 1000000 100000
pwm 1000000 0
```

The LED gets dimmer with each step and then turns off. `breathe 5` fades it
up and down 5 times.

i2c demo:
I2C1 with SCL on GP4 (pin 6) and SDA on GP5 (pin 7). I used a
KX132 sensor.

```bash
i2c scan
i2c get 0x1f 0x13
0x3d
i2c set addr reg val...
accel 10 # 10 readings
```

`scan` should show `1f`. Register 0x13 is WHO_AM_I (check the KX132 TRM for details).
`accel` prints the x, y, z acceleration in mg.

spi demo:
SPI2 loopback, like spidev_test on Linux. It sends 32 bytes
and prints what was sent and received.

```bash
$ spi
```

With nothing connected RX is all `00`. Connect GP7 (pin 10) to GP8
(pin 11) and RX matches TX.

xv6 is a re-implementation of Dennis Ritchie's and Ken Thompson's Unix
Version 6 (v6).  xv6 loosely follows the structure and style of v6,
but is implemented for a modern RISC-V multiprocessor using ANSI C.

ACKNOWLEDGMENTS

xv6 is inspired by John Lions's Commentary on UNIX 6th Edition (Peer
to Peer Communications; ISBN: 1-57398-013-7; 1st edition (June 14,
2000)).  See also https://pdos.csail.mit.edu/6.1810/, which provides
pointers to on-line resources for v6.

